<section class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>How do I find the number of days between two dates?</h3>
    <p>Enter the first date in the starting date field. Set the operation to subtract, then enter the second date as a number of days. Alternatively, many people use this tool the other way around: set the start date, add zero days, and then manually adjust until they reach the target date. A cleaner approach is coming in a future update that will add a dedicated "difference between two dates" mode.</p>

    <h3>Does this account for leap years?</h3>
    <p>Yes. The calculator uses the full Gregorian calendar including all leap year rules. Dates around February 29 are handled correctly whether or not the year in question has a leap day.</p>

    <h3>Can I use this for business days only?</h3>
    <p>The calculator adds calendar days by default. Weekends are part of the total count. If you need only business days, count the result and mentally subtract the number of full weekends in the range, or divide the day count by 7 and subtract 2 days per week as a rough estimate.</p>

    <h3>What is the maximum date range I can calculate?</h3>
    <p>The tool supports up to 1,000 years forward or backward and up to 365,000 days, which covers virtually any practical use case including historical date research.</p>
</section>

